<h2>The prosecution&rsquo;s case</h2>
<p>The applicant had been in custody since 24 June 2026 in FIR No. 278/2026 of PS Adarsh Nagar. The prosecution&rsquo;s version was that a child in conflict with law fired shots at the victim — the shots misfired — and that the applicant was the second of two boys in orange t-shirts seen in CCTV footage, who turned back into a lane before the firing. The victim did not know the applicant, and no test identification parade was ever held. When the matter reached the High Court, the Additional Public Prosecutor fairly stated that the CCTV footage and the child&rsquo;s disclosure statement were the only material connecting the applicant to the incident.</p>

<h2>The Court watches the footage</h2>
<p>Rather than deciding on descriptions of the footage in the status report, Justice Girish Kathpalia had the CCTV footage played in court. What it showed settled the application: the person identified as the applicant appeared only up to chest level, and his face was not visible at any point in the recording. Footage of that kind cannot, on its own, identify anyone.</p>
<div class="check">
  <p>The combination that led to bail:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>CCTV footage in which the applicant&rsquo;s face never appears;</li>
    <li>no test identification parade, in a case where the victim did not know the accused;</li>
    <li>a disclosure statement of a co-accused child as the only other material; and</li>
    <li>custody of almost five months with trial yet to come.</li>
  </ul>
</div>
<p>Finding no reason to deny further liberty on that record, the Court allowed the application on a personal bond of Rs 10,000 with one surety.</p>

<h2>Identification evidence at the bail stage</h2>
<p>The decision reflects a consistent judicial approach: where the accused is a stranger to the victim, identification is the backbone of the prosecution case, and the court examines at the bail stage whether that backbone prima facie exists. CCTV footage has become the default identification tool in urban investigations, but it is only as good as what it captures. Footage that shows clothing and build without a face invites exactly the scrutiny applied here — particularly when the investigating agency has also skipped the test identification parade that could have supplied the missing link.</p>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Step one — examine what the footage actually shows.</strong> Defence counsel should insist on viewing the footage rather than accepting the status report&rsquo;s characterisation of it; courts can and do play it in the courtroom.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Step two — map the identification chain.</strong> Who identified the accused, when, and by what means? An absent TIP, a first-time dock identification, or identification only through a disclosure statement each weakens the chain.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Step three — place the custody period against the material.</strong> The weaker the identification, the harder prolonged custody is to justify.</div>
</div>
<div class="note">Section 109 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 corresponds to Section 307 IPC (attempt to murder); Section 61 to Section 120B IPC (criminal conspiracy); and Section 3(5) to Section 34 IPC (common intention). Section 95 BNS — hiring or employing a child to commit an offence — is a provision with no exact IPC equivalent and reflects the new code&rsquo;s specific attention to the use of minors in crime. Observations in a bail order do not decide the trial, and every case turns on its own record.</div>
